        .IFN    0
;**** NOTE : THE PREVIOUS ".IFN 0" MUST BE AFTER THE DEFINITIONS BUT
                BEFORE THE FOLLOWING PANIC EXPLANATORY TEXT.   IT
                PLUS AN "ENDC" AS THE LAST LINE IN THE FILE DEFINES
                THE REMAINDER OF THE FILE AS TEXT
                PANIC CODE INTRODUCTION
                -----------------------

        PANIC CODES HAVE NOW BEEN MADE INTO UNIQUE CODES.   EACH
PANIC CODE CONSISTS OF A MAJOR CODE(BITS 0-6)/MINOR CODE(BITS 7-15)
FORMAT.   FOR EXAMPLE, A PANIC "007002" IS A MAJOR CODE 7, MINOR
CODE 2.   WITH THIS UNIQUENESS, EACH PANIC CODE CAN BE QUICKLY RE-
SOLVED TO A "PANIC CAUSE" AND A DETERMINATION MADE OF FURTHER ACTION.

        THE PANIC MODULE WILL NOW ALWAYS TYPE OUT TWELVE (12) VALUES ON A
PANIC.   THE FIRST THREE VALUES, 1-3, WILL ALWAYS BE TYPED OUT WHETHER
THEY ARE MEANINGFUL OR NOT, AND WILL BE THE AC0-AC2 VALUES PASSED TO
THE "PANIC" MODULE (EXCEPT FOR PANICS AND DISK ERRORS).   VALUES
4-8 MAY OR MAY NOT BE USED.   IF THEY ARE UNUSED, THEY WILL CONTAIN
"0".  VALUES 9-12 (AOS/VS ONLY) ARE THE HARDWARE STACK POINTER, FRAME
POINTER, STACK LIMIT AND STACK BASE, IN THAT ORDER, AT THE TIME OF THE
PANIC.   REFER TO THE DESCRIPTIONS IN THE FOLLOWING SECTIONS FOR EACH
RELEVANT VALUE FOR THE RESPECTIVE PANIC.   ANY VALUES THAT ARE NOT
RELEVANT WILL NOT BE EXPLAINED.

        NOTE THAT THE VALUE OF AC3 AT THE "XJSR PNIC" IS NO LONGER
TYPED OUT IN A PANIC MESSAGE.   IT IS SAVED FOR DEBUGGING IN A GLOBAL
LOCATION, "PANPC.W".
PANIC ROUTINE           PANIC DESCRIPTION
----- -------           ------------------------------------

;****** PANIC CODE 1 ******


1300     OVFLO                  AN AOS/VS INTERRUPT STACK FAULT HAS
                                  OCCURRED.
                                
                                  VALUE #1= WIDE STACK POINTER
                                  VALUE #2= WIDE FRAME POINTER
                                  VALUE #3= WIDE STACK LIMIT
                                  VALUE #4= WIDE STACK BASE
                                  VALUE #5= PC OF INSTRUCTION THAT
                                    CAUSED FAULT
                                  VALUE #6= STACK FAULT CODE (SEE HDW SPEC)
                                  VALUE #7= CURRENT INTERRUPT LEVEL
                                                (INTLV)

1301     SSOVF                  AN AOS/VS SYSTEM STACK FAULT HAS
                                  OCCURRED.
                                  
                                  VALUE #1= WIDE STACK POINTER
                                  VALUE #2= WIDE FRAME POINTER
                                  VALUE #3= WIDE STACK LIMIT
                                  VALUE #4= WIDE STACK BASE
                                  VALUE #5= PC OF INSTRUCTION THAT
                                    CAUSED FAULT
                                  VALUE #6= STACK FAULT CODE 
                                  VALUE #7= (UNUSED)


                                STACK FAULT CODES:
                                0 OVERFLOW ON ANY STACK OPERATION EXCEPT
                                  SAVE, WMSP, OR RING CROSSING

                                1 UNDERFLOW OR OVERFLOW WOULD OCCUR IF THE
                                  INSTRUCTIONS WMSP, WSAVR OR WSAVS WERE
                                  EXECUTED.  THE PC IN THE RETURN BLOCK
                                  REFERENCES THE INSTRUCTION WHICH CAUSED
                                  THE STACK FAULT

                                2 TOO MANY ARGUMENTS ON A CROSS RING CALL

                                3 STACK UNDERFLOW

                                4 OVERFLOW CAUSED BY A RETURN BLOCK PUSHED
                                  DURING SERVICE OF A MICROINTERRUPT OR 
                                  FAULT


1302    SSOVFE                  AN ECLIPSE STACK FAULT HAS OCCURRED
                                 IN AN AOS/VS SYSTEM. THIS WILL OCCUR
                                 IF ANY ECLIPSE STACK OPERATION IS 
                                 ATTEMPTED. THE ECLIPSE STACK IS LOCATED
                                 AT THE TOP OF THE SYSTEM PG0 STARTING
                                 AT LOCATION 370. THE STACK FAULT BLOCK
                                 WILL BE PUSHED HERE.

                                VALUE #1= AC0 AT TIME OF FAULT
                                VALUE #2= AC1 AT TIME OF FAULT
                                VALUE #3= AC2 AT TIME OF FAULT
                                VALUE #4= AC3 AT TIME OF FAULT
                                VALUE #5= 16 BIT PC AT TIME OF FAULT
                                VALUES #6,7,8 - UNUSED



A COUPLE GENERAL NOTES:
        A)  WHEN THE HARDWARE TRIGGERS A STACK OVERFLOW,  BIT 0
                OF THE STACK LIMIT IS SET TO "1".   THEREFORE IT
                SHOULD BE FOUND SET TO "1" IN VALUE #3.

        B)  IF AN INTERRUPT OCCURS, THE FRAME POINTER WILL USUALLY
                BE FOUND TO BE "0" UNTIL THE 1ST "SAVE" IS DONE.

        C)  WHEN A SYSTEM STACK IS BEING USED, "SAVE'S" ARE USUALLY
                DONE QUITE FREQUENTLY THEREFORE THE SP (VALUE #1)
                SHOULD BE CLOSE TO THE FRAME POINTER (VALUE #2).

        D)  NOTE THAT THE STACK POINTER (VALUE #1) SHOULD BE CLOSE
                TO THE STACK LIMIT (VALUE #3) BY APPROX. 23 MAX
                LOCATIONS.   IF THERE IS A LARGE DIFFERENCE, LOC 40
                MAY HAVE BEEN ACCIDENTLY STORED INTO.
PANIC   ROUTINE         PANIC DESCRIPTION
-----   -------         -------------------------------------

;****** PANIC CODE 2 ******

2001    SOVLY                   AN "I/O ERROR" RETURN HAS BEEN TAKEN
                                  FROM THE WAIT FOR I/O COMPLETION
                                  ROUTINE (BWAIT).  PREVIOUSLY, A
                                  BUFFER HEADER HAD BEEN ENQUEUED
                                  FOR I/O.  THIS IS PROBABLLY A HARD-
                                  DISK SUB-SYSTEM PROBLEM OR A TIMEOUT
                                  ON A COMMAND TO A DISK DRIVE (3 SEC).

                                  VALUE #1= 1ST DISK STATUS WORD
                                                => DIA FOR ALL MHD DISKS
                                                => DIC FOR FHD DISK
                                  VALUE #2= 2ND DISK STATUS WORD
                                                => DIB FOR 6060/61/67
                                                => 0 - ALL OTHERS
                                  VALUE #3= UDB ADDRESS
                                  VALUE #4= DISK UNIT # (HIGH BYTE)
                                    DISK DEVICE CODE (LOW BYTE)
                                  VALUE #5= "DOC" WORD
                                                => DOC ( CYLINDER ADDRESS )
                                                   FOR 6060/61/67
                                                => DOC FOR OTHER MHD'S
                                                => TEMP LOC FOR FHD
                                  VALUE #6= "DOA" WORD
                                                => DOC ( SURFACE,SECTOR,
                                                   SECTOR CNT ) FOR 6060/
                                                   61/67
                                                => DOA FOR OTHER MHD'S
                                                => UNUSED FOR FHD
                                  VALUE #7= TRANSFER ERROR STATUS WORD
                                                1B0 => ERROR
                                                1B1 => "HARD" ERROR
                                                1B2 => DATA LATE
                                                 B12-B15 => # RETRIES
                                  VALUE #8= CONTROL BLOCK "SYSTEM CALL"
                                                ERROR CODE - REFER TO
                                                LIST IN "PARU.SR".
                                
                                SOME OF THE MORE COMMON HARDWARE RELATED
                                "SYSTEM CALL" ERROR CODES ARE:
                                        75  -- FILE READ ERROR
                                        76  -- DEVICE TIMEOUT
                                        121 -- PHYSICAL UNIT FAILURE
                                        123 -- PHYSICAL UNIT OFF-LINE
                                THE OTHERS CAN BE FOUND IN "PARU.SR" .
                                THE VARIOUS HARDWARE STATUS WORDS CAN
                                BE INTERPRETED BY REFERING TO THE
                                APPROPRIATE DISK HARDWARE MANUAL OR TO
                                THE "PROGRAMMER'S REFERENCE - PERIPHERALS"
                                (015-21).

2002    SWAPM                   SAME AS FOR "002001".

2003    SWAPI                   SAME AS FOR "002001".

2004    SWAPI                   THIS PANIC IS CAUSED BY TAKING AN ERROR
                                  RETURN TO THE "JSR@ .BLKIN" INTERNAL
                                  CALL.  THIS CALL IS USED TO READ ONE
                                  BLOCK OF A DISK FILE.  AN ERROR CAN
                                  BE CAUSED BY EITHER:
                                        A) AN "I/O ERROR" ERROR RETURN
                                                FROM "BWAIT"
                                        B) AN ERROR FROM THE DISK WORLD
                                                SOFTWARE (NQCCB)
                                  REFER THE "SYSTEM CALL ERROR CODE"
                                  WHICH IS CONTAINED IN "VALUE #1" TO
                                  TO "PARU.SR" TO DETERMINE WHETHER
                                  THE ERROR CAN BE DIRECTLY RELATED
                                  TO THE HARDWARE DISK SUB-SYSTEM.

                                  VALUE #1= "SYSTEM CALL ERROR CODE"

2005    SWAPM                   SAME AS "002004".

2006    SWAPO                   SAME AS "002004".

2300    READSY                  UNEXPECTED ERROR RETURN FROM BWAIT
                                WHILE READING SYSTEM NON-RESIDENT PAGE
                                VALUE #1 = UDB ADDRESS
                                VALUE #3 = BUFFER HEADER ADDRESS
PANIC   ROUTINE         PANIC DESCRIPTION
-----   -------         -----------------------------------

;****** PANIC CODE 3 ******

THIS PANIC CODE DOES NOT EXIST AT THIS TIME.

PANIC   ROUTINE         PANIC DESCRIPTION
-----   -------         ------------------------------------

;****** PANIC CODE 4 ******

4001    PFL                     THERE HAVE BEEN GREATER THAN 65K
                                  INTERRUPTS FROM DEVICE CODE 0
                                  (OR SPURIOUS INTERRUPTS) SINCE
                                  AOS/VS WAS BOOTED.  THE LOCATION
                                  "PCNT" WAS ISZ'D FOR EACH DEVICE
                                  CODE 0 INTERRUPT AND WRAPPED AROUND.

4002    IUD                     AN INTERRUPT HAS OCCURRED WHICH CAN
                                  NOT BE CLEARED VIA AN "NIOC" AND
                                  WHICH WAS UNDEFINED IN "ITBL".
                                  THERE HAVE BEEN 2000. TRIES TO
                                  CLEAR THE INTERRUPT.

                                  VALUE #3= DEVICE CODE OF THE DEVICE
                                                WHICH INTERRUPTED



        NOTE THAT TWO ADDITIONAL GLOBAL VARIABLES CAN BE USEFUL
                WHEN TROUBLESHOOTING PROBLEMS INVOLVING UNDEFINED
                INTERRUPTS.   THESE ARE:

                "UNDDC" -- WILL CONTAIN THE DEVICE CODE OF THE LAST
                                UNDEFINED INTERRUPT
                "UNCNT" -- WILL CONTAIN A COUNT OF THE NUMBER OF 
                                UNDEFINED INTERRUPTS THAT HAVE OCCURRED
                                SINCE THE SYSTEM WAS BOOTED.   THIS
                                VALUE IS A SINGLE WORD AND WILL WRAP
                                AROUND TO "0" AFTER 65K UNDEFINED
                                INTERRUPTS, IE: MOD 65K.

        THE VALUE UNCNT CAN BE USEFUL, SINCE IT CAN BE "MONITORED"
        WHILE THE SYSTEM IS RUNNING (ON AN ECLIPSE), IN DETERMINING
        WHETHER A SYSTEM "SLOW-DOWN" IS DUE TO AN UNDEFINED DEVICE
        WHICH IS CLEARABLE (THEREFORE NO PANIC) CONTINUOUSLY INTER-
        RUPTING.


4003    OUTCM                   THE OUTPUT A COMMAND ROUTINE (IN DPFDR)
                                LOOPS CHECKING THE CONTROLLER-FULL BIT OF
                                THE DIA BEFORE GOING AHEAD WITH THE DOA.
                                THIS LOOP HAS TIMED OUT THE DISK AND WAS 
                                ALSO UNABLE TO CLEAR THE BIT WITH AN NIOC. 

PANIC   ROUTINE         PANIC DESCRIPTION
------  -------         -------------------------------------

;****** PANIC CODE 5 ******

5001     IDSWP                  AN EVENT HAS CAUSED THE RESPECTIVE
                                  PROCESS'S DISK "SWAP" AREA TO HAVE TO
                                  BE CHANGED.   FIRST THE SYSTEM
                                  WILL RELEASE THE OLD "SWAP" AREA
                                  THEN ATTEMPT TO FIND ANOTHER AREA
                                  LARGE ENOUGH TO CONTAIN THE NEW
                                  PROCESS SIZE.   IN THIS CASE, THE
                                  OLD "SWAP" AREA IS BEING RELEASED
                                  AND THE SWAP FILE BIT MAP INDICATES
                                  THAT THE AREA HAD BEEN MARKED AS
                                  NOT "IN-USE"  -- A DEFINITE INCON-
                                  SISTANCY.

                                  VALUE #1= NEG # BITS FOR SWAP AREA
                                  VALUE #2= WORD/BIT ADDRESS
                                  VALUE #3= BUFFER HEADER ADDRESS FOR
                                                BIT MAP

5002    IWSWP                   SAME GLOBAL EVENTS AS IN "005001"
                                  EXCEPT THAT NOW THE NEW "SWAP"
                                  AREA IS BEING ASSIGNED.   THIS
                                  AREA HAD BEEN DETERMINED TO BE
                                  FREE BUT WHEN THE ACTUAL ALLOCATION
                                  IS DONE IN THE SWAP FILE "BIT"
                                  MAP, AN AREA IS FOUND ALREADY SET
                                  "IN-USE".

                                  VALUE #1= NEG # BITS FOR SWAP AREA
                                  VALUE #2= FIELD ADDRESS       
                                  VALUE #3= WORD/BIT ADDRESS

        NOTE THAT EACH BIT IN THE SWAP FILE BIT MAP CORRESPONDS
                TO 1 MEMORY PAGE WHICH IS 4 DISK BLOCKS.


PANIC   ROUTINE                 PANIC DESCRIPTION-----  -------                 -----------------------------------

;****** PANIC CODE 6 ******

6001    DRLSE                   A SUBROUTINE HAS BEEN ENTERED TO
                                  RELEASE THE LOGICAL DISK CONTROL
                                  BLOCK (LCB) ASSOCIATED WITH A GIVEN
                                  LOGICAL DISK UNIT (LDU) FROM THE
                                  CHAIN OF INITIALIZED LCB'S IN THE
                                  SYSTEM.   THE LCB TO BE RELEASED
                                  CANNOT BE FOUND ON THE CHAIN.

                                  VALUE #1= ADDR OF LCB TO BE RELEASED
                                  VALUE #3= -1 FOR THE END OF LCB CHAIN

6002    DRLSE                   THE PARENT CHANNEL CONTROL BLOCK
                                  (CCB) IS DECREMENTED TWICE.  IT IS
                                  FOUND THAT THE ORIGINAL CCB "USE
                                  COUNT" WAS "1" INSTEAD OF "2".

                                  VALUE #3= PARENT CCB ADDRESS

6003    ICLOSE                  ON DOING A CLOSE, THE PARENT CCB
                                  ADDRESS IS FOUND TO BE ZERO.

6004    KFCB                    ON KILLING AN FCB, BOTH THE FCB
                                  OPEN COUNT AND THE PARENT CCB USE
                                  COUNT ARE DECREMENTED.  AFTER THE
                                  PARENT CCB USE COUNT IS DECREMENTED,
                                  IT IS FOUND TO BE ZERO.

                                  VALUE #3= PARENT CCB ADDRESS

6005    KCCB                    ON KILLING A CCB, THE PARENT CCB USE
                                  COUNT IS DECREMENTED BY ONE.  AFTER
                                  BEING DECREMENTED, IT IS FOUND TO
                                  BE ZERO WHICH SHOULD NEVER HAPPEN
                                  SINCE THE "SON FCB" SHOULD STILL
                                  EXIST.

                                  VALUE #3=  PARENT CCB ADDRESS

6006    KCCB                    THE SYSTEM IS ATTEMPTING TO FLUSH
                                  A "FIB" WHILE "I/O IN PROG" IS
                                  ALREADY SET.

                                  VALUE #1= 40
                                  VALUE #2= FCB STATUS (FBSTS)
                                  VALUE #3= FCB ADDRESS

6007    SYDBL                   WHILE RELEASING THE CCB HIERARCHY,
                                  IT IS FOUND THAT THE PARENT CCB
                                  POINTER IS ZERO.

6010    XINIT                   SAME REASON AND VALUES AS "006002".

6011    XINIT                   SAME REASON AS "006001".

                                  VALUE #1= LCB ADDR TO BE REMOVED
                                  VALUE #3= 177777 (END OF CHAIN)

6012                            RESERVED

6013                            RESERVED

6014    VDETAS/RAID             DIRECTORY ELEMENT HAD INVALID SIZE,
                                  WAS NOT A MULT. OF 8.

6015                            RESERVED

6016    ESD                     INVALID LINK WORD FOUND WHILE VALIDATING
                                  A LINKED CHAIN

                                  VALUE #1= ADDR OF QUEUE DESCRIPTOR
                                  VALUE #3= INVALID LINK WORD

6017    ESD                     MAXIMUM # OF ENTRIES IN CHAIN HAVE BEEN
                                  VALIDATED BY NO TERMINATOR HAS BEEN
                                  FOUND

                                  VALUE #1= ADDR OF QUEUE DESCRIPTOR

6020                            RESERVED

6021                            RESERVED

6022    LINKS                   A FILENAME IN A BLOCK WHICH WAS JUST
                                  RAID'ED IN WAS FOUND TO CONTAIN MORE
                                  THAN 31. CHARACTERS.

                                  VALUE #1 = BUFFER HEADER ADDRESS
                                  VALUE #2 = BP INTO NAMSP.W
                                  VALUE #3 = BP INTO FILENAME OF AN
                                             FNB IN THE BUFFER WHICH IS
                                             MORE THAN 31. CHARACTERS.


6023    RESLV                   IN RESOLVING A PATHNAME WE ENCOUNTERED
                                  A FILE OF TYPE ?FMDR, THIS IS NOT
                                  SUPPORTED BY AOS/VS.  FIXUP WILL
                                  DELETE THE FILE AS A TEMP FILE.

                                  VALUE #1 = FILE TYPE
                                  VALUE #3 = FIB BUFFER ADDRESS

6024                            RESERVED

6025    FFCB                    WE DO NOT HAVE AN FCB FOR AN OPEN FILE
                                  (WE DO HAVE A CHANNEL FOR THE FILE).
                                  THE FCB ADDRESS IN THE FIB IN MEMORY
                                  IS ZERO.
                                
                                  VALUE #1 = PARENT CCB POINTER
                                  VALUE #2 = 1B0 (FLAG THAT SHOWS WE
                                             HAVE A CHANNEL)
                                  VALUE #3 = FCB ADDRESS FROM THE FIB
                                             IN MEMORY ( = 0 ).

6026    JELLO.P                 INVALID DIRECTORY ELEMENT SIZE REQUEST

                                  VALUE # 2 = ELEMENT SIZE REQUESTED
                                  VALUE # 3 = DIRECTORY CCB ADDRESS

6027    VDETAS                  INVALID DIRECTORY ELEMENT TYPE
                                
                                  VALUE #1 = DIRECTORY ELEMENT SIZE
                                  VALUE #2 = DIRECTORY ELEMENT TYPE
                                               (BITS 16-23)

6030    REDEL                   UNEXPECTED FREE ELEMENT

                                  VALUE #1 = DETAS WORD OF ELEMENT
                                  VALUE #2 = DIRECTORY ELEMENT'S
                                             BUFFER HEADER ADDRESS
                                  VALUE #3 = ADDRESS OF DIRECTORY
                                             ELEMENT IN SYSTEM

6031    RAID                    INVALID DIRECTORY DATA BLOCK, THE
                                  NEXT OR PREVIOUS RELATIVE BLOCK #
                                  IN OFFSETS 0/1 IS INVALID.

                                  VALUE #1 = PREVIOUS AND NEXT
                                             RELATIVE BLOCK NUMBERS.    
                                        BITS 0-15 = NEXT DDB
                                        BITS 16-31 = PREVIOUS DDB
                                  VALUE #3 = DIRECTORY DATA BLOCK ADDR.

6032    RAID                    REQUESTED DIRECTORY ELEMENT TYPE
                                  NOT FOUND

                                  VALUE #1 = DIRECTORY ELEMENT SIZE
                                  VALUE #2 = DIRECTORY ELEMENT TYPE
                                             REQUESTED
                                  VALUE #3 = DIRECTORY ELEMENT TYPE
                                             FOUND


6033    CCHN.P                  WE DO NOT HAVE AN FCB FOR AN OPEN FILE
                                  (WE DO HAVE A CHANNEL FOR THE FILE).
                                  THE FCB ADDRESS IN THE FIB IN MEMORY
                                  IS ZERO.

                                  VALUE #1 = 1B30 (FLAG THAT SHOWS WE
                                             HAVE A CHANNEL)
                                  VALUE #3 = ADDRESS OF FIB IN MEMORY

6034    VALCID                  VALCID FOUND THE CCB UID DOES NOT MATCH
                                  THE FCB UID.

                                  VALUE #1 = CCB UID
                                  VALUE #2 = FCB UID
                                  VALUE #3 = FCB ADDRESS OBTAINED FROM
                                             CCB PASSED IN.


;
; AOS/VS SPECIFIC PANICS
;

6302    PPCUS                   USER CCB POST PROCESSOR WENT TO UNLOCK
                                  CCB, BUT IT ALREADY WAS.

                                  VALUE # 3 = CCB ADDRESS

6303    NQCCB                   LPHY FAILED ON CCB ADDRESS

                                  VALUE # 1 = LAST RESIDENT PTE
                                  VALUE # 2 = CCB ADDRESS

6304    MQCCB                   LPHY FAILED ON CCB ADDRESS

                                  VALUE # 1 = LAST RESIDENT PTE
                                  VALUE # 2 = CCB ADDRESS

6305    LPBIO/LPDIO             LPHY FAILED ON CCB ADDRESS

                                  VALUE # 1 = LAST RESIDENT PTE
                                  VALUE # 2 = CCB ADDRESS

6306    MCACB                   LPHY FAILED ON CCB ADDRESS

                                  VALUE # 1 = LAST RESIDENT PTE
                                  VALUE # 2 = CCB ADDRESS

6307    OFAULT.P                UNEXPECTED ERROR RETURN FROM MCLR

                                  VALUE # 3 = ADDR OF CCB BEING CLEARED

6313    DSKIO                   AN UNEXPECTED 'HOLE' WAS FOUND IN A FILE
                                  AT THE POINT OF OCCURENCE, WE SHOULD
                                  HAVE ALREADY GROWN THE FILE

                                  VALUE #2 = ADDR OF INDEX BLOCK WHICH
                                             CONTAINED 'HOLE' (SHOULD
                                             BE OFFSET ZERO IN BLOCK)

6314    DSKIO                   A FILE TRUNCATE RQ WAS BEING PROCESSED
                                  THAT SPECIFIED A BLOCK CNT <> 0.

                                  VALUE # 1 = # BLKS SPECIFIED
                                  VALUE # 3 = IOCB ADDRESS

6315    DSKIO                   A READ SYSTEM RQ WAS BEING PROCESSED
                                  THAT SPECIFIED A BLOCK CNT <>1, OR A
                                  DBL PRECISION STARTING BLOCK #.
 
                                  VALUE # 3 = IOCB ADDRESS

6324    WDCBK                   WDCBK ATTEMPTING TO ALLOCATE LOGICAL
                                  BLOCK 0

                                  VALUE # 1 = 0
                                  VALUE # 3 = LCB ADDRESS

6325    DEBKS                   DEBKS ATTEMPTING TO DE-ALLOCATE LOGICAL
                                  BLOCK 0

                                  VALUE # 1 = 0
                                  VALUE # 3 = IOCB ADDRESS

6331    OPEN                    CCB USE COUNT WENT TO ZERO AFTER UNPEND
                                  WAITING SYSTEM EXCLUSIVE OPEN.

                                  VALUE # 2 = FCB ADDRESS
                                  VALUE # 3 = CCB ADDRESS

6332    TRUNCATE                SYSTEM XOPEN BIT ALREADY SET.

                                  VALUE # 3 = CCB ADDRESS

6333    TRUNCATE                ERROR TAKEN FROM FLFCWL CALL AFTER PART
                                  2 TRUNCATE, SO CAN'T ALLOW WITHDRAW
                                  BLOCKS

                                  VALUE # 1 = ERROR CODE
                                  VALUE # 3 = CCB ADDRESS


6335    WDCBK                   WDCBK IS GOING TO WAIT FOR A BIT MAP TO
                                  BE UNLOCKED. IF WE'RE IN ESD THAT
                                  WON'T HAPPEN SO WE PANIC, BELIEVING
                                  THAT A PANIC IS BETTER THAN A HANG.
                                  (ESD CAN GET TO WDCBK VIA SYSLOG, IF
                                  LOGGING A PANIC REQUIRES AN ADDITIONAL
                                  BLOCK FOR THE LOG FILE.)

                                  VALUE # 4 = LCB ADDRESS

6336    IRLSE.P                 THE CCB PASSED TO IRLSE.P DOES NOT
                                  BELONG TO AN LDU.

                                  VALUE # 2 = CCB ADDRESS
                                  VALUE # 3 = FCB ADDRESS

6337    GFCB/KPFCB              THESE TWO CALLS WORK AS A PAIR...YOU
                                  CALL GFCB TO FIND SPACE FOR AN FCB,
                                  THEN YOU INITIALIZE IT, THEN YOU CALL
                                  KPFCB TO MARK IT ALLOCATED. IF THERE
                                  ARE TWO GFCB CALLS WITHOUT AN
                                  INTERVENING KPFCB, THINGS WILL GET
                                  SCREWED UP. HERE, WE TOOK TWO OF ONE
                                  CALL WITHOUT AN INTERVENING OTHER CALL
                                  LOOK FOR A PENDABLE PATH BETWEEN GFCB
                                  AND KPFCB.

                                  VALUE # 1 = EXPECTED ENTRY (+1 = KPFCB
                                                              -1 = GFCB)
                                  VALUE # 2 = ACTUAL ENTRY

6340    KPFCB                   KPFCB SHOULD FIND THE FCB JUST FOUND by
                                  GFCB. HERE, IT COULDN'T FIND ANYTHING
                                  THIS PROBABLY MEANS THAT GFCB COULDN'T
                                  FIND A NEW PAGE AND THE CALLER
                                  MISHANDLED THE ERROR.

6342    NQPPBF.P                WE ARE TRYING TO RELEASE A PPB TO THE
                                  FREE LIST ON ITS PAGE AND HAVE FOUND
                                  THAT THE PAGE TO WHICH IT BELONGS IS
                                  NOT A PPB PAGE.

                                  VALUE # 1 = PPB ADDRESS

6346    FFCB                    THE FIB WHICH WE'RE SUPPOSED TO BE OPEN-
                                  ING DOES NOT HAVE A FIB TYPE IN THE
                                  DETAS WORD. THE DISK BLOCK ON THE DISK
                                  IS PROBABLY DESTROYED.

                                  VALUE #3 = FIB ADDRESS

6347    RNA                     AN INDEX BLOCK WAS FOUND WHICH HAS
                                  NOTHING ALLOCATED IN IT.

                                  VALUE #1 = 0
                                  VALUE #2 = 0
                                  VALUE #3 = IOCB ADDRESS

6350    ITRUNC.P                A CALL HAS BEEN MADE TO ITRUNC.P TO
                                  TRUNCATE A SYSTEM FILE (PAGE OR SWAP).
                                  WE WERE NOT SUCCESSFUL IN DOING A
                                  FAULT & WIRE ON THE SYSTEM CCB.

                                  VALUE #1 = CCB ADDRESS
                                  VALUE #2 = PROCESS TABLE ADDRESS

6351    TRUNCATE                A CALL WAS MADE TO UPDATE INDEX BLOCKS
                                  AFTER A PART 1 TRUNCATE, AND THIS TOOK
                                  THE ERROR RETURN.  TO CONTINUE WOULD
                                  MEAN MULTIPLY ALLOCATED BLOCKS COULD
                                  OCCUR.

                                  VALUE #1 = I/O ERROR CODE
                                  VALUE #3 = FCB ADDRESS

6352    DSKIO                   IN TRUNCATE PROCESSING A CALL WAS MADE
                                  TO QIOWT, TO WRITE OUT THE INDEX
                                  BLOCK, AND IT TOOK THE ERROR RETURN.
                                
                                  VALUE #1 = I/O ERROR CODE
                                  VALUE #3 = BH ADDRESS

6353    FCULCK/UCULCK           ATTEMPT TO UNLOCK AN UNLOCKED CCB.
                                  IF FCULCK THE FAULT LOCK IS NOT SET;
                                  IF UCULCK THE VANILLA LOCK IS NOT SET.

                                  VALUE #3 = CCB ADDRESS

6354    UCLOCK                  LPHY FAILED ON A USER CCB ADDRESS

                                  VALUE #1 = LAST RESIDENT PTE
                                  VALUE #2 = CCB ADDRESS
                                  VALUE #3 = RING 1 CCB ADDRESS

6355    CLOCK/FCLOCK            CCB FOUND TO HAVE ONLY FAULT BIT SET
                                  WITHOUT THE VANILLA LOCK SET.

                                  VALUE #3 = CCB ADDRESS

6356    SETUP_RECORD            DISPATCH FAILED. ENTRY SWITCH IS OUT
                                  OF RANGE

                                  VALUE #2 = ENTRY SWITCH

6357    DSKIO                   INVALID DATA ELEMENT SIZE WAS FOUND
                                BEFORE COMPUTING THE NO. OF CONTIGUOUS BLOCKS

                                  VALUE #2 = DATA ELEMENT SIZE
                                  VALUE #3 = IOCB ADDRESS

6360    MIROP.P                 INVALID MDB STATE ENCOUNTERED WHEN
                                ATTEMPTING TO RUN THE MDB REQUEST.

                                  VALUE #1 = INVALID MDB STATE
                                  VALUE #3 = MDB ADDRESS

6361    MIROP.P                 ATTEMPT TO RUN SIMULTANEOUS MURB
                                REQUESTS ON THE SAME MURB.

                                  VALUE #1 = REQUESTED MURB STATE
                                  VALUE #2 = CURRENT MURB STATE
                                  VALUE #3 = MURB ADDRESS

6362    MIROP.P                 INVALID MURB STATE ENCOUNTERED WHEN
                                ATTEMPTING TO RUN THE MURB REQUEST.

                                  VALUE #1 = INVALID MURB STATE
                                  VALUE #3 = MURB ADDRESS

6363    MIRER.P                 TRIED TO RELEASE A MIRROR UDB WHEN
                                THE MIRROR WAS NOT BROKEN.

                                  VALUE #1 = MIRROR STATE
                                  VALUE #3 = MIRROR UDB

6364    MIRER.P                 TRIED TO RELEASE THE MDB FOR THE
